{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790256002,"nonce":"pya/ebvzW8vAIYn66nlThPY51Us=","message":"Two separate platform causes, both in how the walk reads a frame, neither reproducible on a macOS 26 machine.\n\nWindows: LLVM's Win64 prologue sets rbp = rsp + offset, so [rbp] is not the saved frame-pointer/return-address pair. 041bf94 gives LLVM bodies a frame record there; the windows-2022 LLVM tier has been green since (571b93f, 5b03831).\n\nmacOS: cranelift-native enables sign_return_address with the B key on macOS, so every Cranelift prologue starts with pacibsp and the saved return address carries a signature above bit 46. The macos-14 (M1) runners apply the signature; a macOS 26 machine runs plain arm64 processes with the keys off, so the same binary reads a clean address here and a signed one there. 42d24dc strips the bits above the user address space when the saved word names no compiled body.\n\nBoth have a regression test that fails without the fix: a_frame_names_its_body_through_a_signed_return_address and the trace tests, which now print the walk and each LLVM compile's captured sections when they fail.","files":null}]}